            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes a tensor of random samples from the EXPONENTIAL distribution: the time you wait for something that happens at a steady random rate.\n\nShort waits are the most likely, long ones rarer and rarer, each extra moment of waiting as likely to end the wait as the last. Use it for the gaps between random events - clicks of a Geiger counter, raindrops, the timing of generated notes - or for positive noise that is usually small.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.exponential <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.exponential 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.exponential 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\nrate (drag float, also an inlet):\nHow often the events happen, per unit of time. Must be above 0. The average wait is 1 / rate, so a higher rate means shorter waits. Default 1.\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A parameter that must be above 0 has a drag float that stops at 0.000001. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) - 0 or below sent into such an inlet, say - prints a message in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last parameters that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. The heat_map (0 dark, 3 bright) is mostly dark with brighter specks. The histogram is tallest at 0 and falls away in a smooth curve, halving every 0.69 / rate. Raise rate and it crowds against 0; lower it and the curve stretches out to the right.\n\nRELATED:\nt.exponential fills an existing tensor with the same distribution. t.dist.poisson counts how many such events land in a fixed time. t.dist.gamma is the wait for several events; t.dist.weibull generalises this curve; t.dist.laplace is two of them back to back.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
